WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
Two tubes of spun blood.

2
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:07.900
An endurance athlete can show the shorter red layer and still carry more red cells

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.100
than an untrained friend.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.620
How does one number fall while the cells rise?

5
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:21.607
Hematocrit is a ratio: the volume of packed red cells over the volume of whole

6
00:00:21.607 --> 00:00:25.847
blood. Take made-up round numbers for an untrained adult.

7
00:00:27.187 --> 00:00:31.027
2.2 L of red cells, 2.8 L of plasma.

8
00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:33.967
44 percent.

9
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.170
A ratio answers to its top and its bottom separately.

10
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:40.970
Add red cells and it rises.

11
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.630
Add plasma and it falls.

12
00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.790
And nothing in the body regulates the ratio itself.

13
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:53.493
Pregnancy expands plasma more than red cells, so hematocrit falls.

14
00:00:54.853 --> 00:00:58.013
In the first days at altitude it runs the other way.

15
00:00:59.193 --> 00:01:04.673
A diuresis shrinks plasma, and hematocrit rises before red cell mass has changed

16
00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:05.673
much.

17
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:08.633
So a concentration cannot give you a mass.

18
00:01:09.873 --> 00:01:15.493
For that you need plasma volume by indicator dilution, or hemoglobin mass by carbon

19
00:01:15.493 --> 00:01:16.793
monoxide rebreathing.

20
00:01:17.913 --> 00:01:20.353
The spun tube reports a share, never a count.

21
00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:22.897
Here is the one idea.

22
00:01:24.197 --> 00:01:26.517
Nothing in the body regulates that ratio.

23
00:01:27.777 --> 00:01:32.877
The kidney regulates its numerator, the red cells, and it does that by reading the

24
00:01:32.877 --> 00:01:34.777
oxygen in its own patch of tissue.

25
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:41.010
Those readers are fibroblast-like interstitial cells, sitting between the kidney

26
00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.870
tubules rather than in them.

27
00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:49.730
They lie in a relatively low-oxygen zone, so a small fall in blood oxygen content

28
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.910
lowers their oxygen enough to matter.

29
00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:57.273
The switch inside them is HIF-2 alpha.

30
00:01:58.673 --> 00:02:04.473
In a well-oxygenated cell, hydroxylase enzymes that need oxygen, iron and

31
00:02:04.473 --> 00:02:06.493
2-oxoglutarate mark it for destruction.

32
00:02:07.873 --> 00:02:12.213
When oxygen falls, it accumulates and turns on the erythropoietin gene.

33
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:18.893
In lowlanders breathing thin air, plasma erythropoietin rises within the first day

34
00:02:18.893 --> 00:02:22.133
or two. The new red cells arrive later.

35
00:02:23.453 --> 00:02:25.173
Why does the cell count lag the hormone?

36
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.460
Erythropoietin acts mainly by preventing the death of late red cell progenitors.

37
00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.020
Those cells still have to divide and mature.

38
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.780
Release of reticulocytes, young red cells that still hold RNA, rises over the

39
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:47.780
following days.

40
00:02:48.633 --> 00:02:54.093
So two unrelated events can move the same number, and the body is not aiming at that

41
00:02:54.093 --> 00:02:59.813
number. A kidney cell orders red cells for the tissue it can feel, and the ratio

42
00:02:59.813 --> 00:03:00.333
follows.
